  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Fall-like pattern into midweek reverts to \nwarmer, more humid/typical late summer conditions to close out \nAugust\n\nOne more Fall-like day with cooler-than-average daytime highs \nas the upper level trough starts to lift out of the area. We \nstill can't rule out a spotty, quickly passing light rain shower\nor brief sprinkles mainly over the higher terrain north/west of\nI99 and north of I80 through the afternoon. Relatively cool, \ncyclonic/northwest flow aloft will continue to generate clouds \ndownwind of Lake Erie and therefore will hedge sky cover upward \nvs. guidance. \n\nClearing skies tonight will favor fog formation into Wednesday \nmorning especially in the valleys as the ground remains rather\nmoist from recent rainfall. Warmer on Wednesday with more \nsunshine and highs +5-10F higher vs. Tuesday. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms with \nlocally heavy rain are possible Thursday afternoon \n\n*SPC introduced level 1/5 severe t-storm risk for Thursday\n\nA broad mid-level trough will continue to shift eastward toward the\nNortheast on Thursday. A weak surface low will move across Quebec\nwith a cold front extending southward into the eastern Great \nLakes. Moderate instability is forecast in advance of the front\nwith broad ascent supporting storm development. Multicell \nclusters capable of damaging winds, and perhaps isolated large \nhail, are expected. A more focused region of greater potential \nmay exist from central Pennsylvania into eastern New York. The \npotential for cloud cover across the region casts some \nuncertainty and therefore, confidence was not high enough for a \nSlight Risk (level 2/5) at this time. WPC continues to highlight\nthe eastern half of the forecast area with a level 1/marginal\nrisk excessive rain outlook with PWATs fcst to climb to 1.5 \ninches in advance of the aforementioned cold front.",
